There are policies and procedures relating to all post-mortem examinations (coroner’s cases);
Persons who are authorised to order for a post-mortem examination; 
Written orders for a medico-legal post-mortem examination
Identification of body part or the deceased by full name*/identification document number/passport/police report number/post-mortem number and/or medical record number; 
Name and rank of the officer ordering the forensic post-mortem examination; 
Retention of records and specimens; 
Types of specimens collected at post-mortem which are to be submitted for histopathological, microbiological/virological, toxicological and other relevant investigation.
Papua New Guinea’s Coroners Act 1953 (Chapter 32)

There are appropriate areas for the following;
Body receiving area shall be of a suitable size and design to facilitate incoming and outgoing of bodies; 
Clean and dirty areas are clearly designated; 
There is sufficient space and refrigeration for storage of bodies with provision for accurate identification of bodies; 
The temperature of the body freezer (2-8⁰C ± 2) shall be maintained, monitored and documented; 
There are adequate space, facilities and equipment for the administrative, professional, and technical functions of the Mortuary Services.

There are appropriate areas for the post-mortem room (where applicable), which include;
Access to the post-mortem room shall be controlled
The post-mortem room is clean and has adequate space, ventilation and lighting; 
There are adequate facilities for performing post-mortem examination, recording of findings, specimen handling and storage; 
The post-mortem equipment is appropriate, adequate and is properly maintained; 
Post-mortem table is of a suitable design with proper facilities for the disposal of effluent into the sewage system to ensure safety; 
There is adequate ventilation with extraction for fumes and odours in the work area where appropriate

There is suitable, adequate and safe provision for;
Lighting, power, water, and drainage, appropriate to the scope of services provided which include;
Power supply, which shall be adequate, and there are sufficient power sockets which are suitably located. 
Adequate and appropriate lighting.

Specific performance indicators are tracked for e.g.;
Turnaround time for release of bodies to the next of kin (non-police) cases is less than 3 hours
Post mortems reports are completed within 12 weeks of the post mortem.
